WEBVTT

NOTE English translation of the Vietnamese narration script. Timing is distributed proportionally within each slide segment, since the narration itself is in Vietnamese.

1
00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:05.304
Hello everyone, this is the revision video for Chapter Four: Business negotiation.

2
00:00:05.304 --> 00:00:09.600
This is probably the most directly profitable skill you'll learn in this course.

3
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.376
Negotiation is a process in which parties,

4
00:00:14.376 --> 00:00:17.092
who share some interests and conflict on others,

5
00:00:17.092 --> 00:00:22.013
exchange views and persuade each other to reach an agreement that all sides can accept.

6
00:00:22.013 --> 00:00:24.730
Its nature is dual: cooperating to grow the pie,

7
00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:26.768
but competing over how it's divided.

8
00:00:26.768 --> 00:00:30.938
The three sources of power at the negotiating table are information, time,

9
00:00:30.938 --> 00:00:34.600
and leverage — meaning who has more alternatives to fall back on.

10
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:38.818
There are three negotiation styles.

11
00:00:38.818 --> 00:00:41.386
The soft style treats the other side as a friend,

12
00:00:41.386 --> 00:00:44.948
giving in easily to preserve the relationship, but often losing out.

13
00:00:44.948 --> 00:00:50.040
The hard style treats the other side as an opponent — it may win once but breaks the relationship.

14
00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:54.407
The principled style, also known as the Harvard style, is the one we should follow:

15
00:00:54.407 --> 00:00:56.354
separate the people from the problem,

16
00:00:56.354 --> 00:01:00.669
focus on interests rather than dig into positions, invent options for mutual gain,

17
00:01:00.669 --> 00:01:02.300
and rely on objective criteria.

18
00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:10.051
The negotiation process has five stages: preparation, opening, bargaining, closing,

19
00:01:10.051 --> 00:01:11.728
and after the negotiation.

20
00:01:11.728 --> 00:01:15.753
Preparation alone decides about seventy percent of the outcome.

21
00:01:15.753 --> 00:01:18.500
Failing to prepare is preparing to concede.

22
00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:23.230
Three concepts you must know by heart.

23
00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:25.085
Three levels of target: ideal,

24
00:01:25.085 --> 00:01:30.649
expected and minimum — the walk-away point — write these down before you sit at the table.

25
00:01:30.649 --> 00:01:35.723
BATNA is your Best Alternative To a Negotiated Agreement; the stronger your BATNA,

26
00:01:35.723 --> 00:01:37.394
the stronger your position.

27
00:01:37.394 --> 00:01:40.062
And ZOPA is the Zone Of Possible Agreement,

28
00:01:40.062 --> 00:01:42.667
the overlap between the two sides' limits.

29
00:01:42.667 --> 00:01:46.684
If there's no ZOPA, don't force the price — grow the pie instead,

30
00:01:46.684 --> 00:01:50.700
by changing the quantity, the schedule, or adding extra services.

31
00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:57.138
At the negotiating table you'll meet a few common tactics: an aggressive anchor,

32
00:01:57.138 --> 00:02:01.126
good cop/bad cop, a fake deadline, “salami slicing” — asking for a little more,

33
00:02:01.126 --> 00:02:01.681
bit by bit,

34
00:02:01.681 --> 00:02:05.770
after the deal is already agreed — and a last-minute demand right before signing.

35
00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:09.884
The general response is to hold to objective criteria, package all terms together,

36
00:02:09.884 --> 00:02:14.400
and always put a price on every request so you can ask for something equivalent in return.

37
00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:18.181
Three things to remember.

38
00:02:18.181 --> 00:02:21.442
A negotiation is won before you even sit down at the table.

39
00:02:21.442 --> 00:02:24.260
Negotiate over interests, not entrenched positions.

40
00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:28.716
And an agreement is only safe once it's put into a properly formatted contract —

41
00:02:28.716 --> 00:02:31.222
which is exactly the subject of Chapter Five.

42
00:02:31.222 --> 00:02:35.035
Go ahead and do the forty multiple-choice questions for Chapter Four.
